        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>The fireplace in the living room is allowing a tremendous amount of unwanted air into the home. This is the cause of the heat distribution problems you are having in the winter. If this isn't being used very often than it should be closed off. If this is closed off then the amount of air leakage into the home will decrease significantly.



You probably will cut down on oil consumption and use of the oil monitor by sealing the firplace properly.



There are air leaks around both front and back doors as well. Replacing the weather stripping and door sweeps would help properly seal the gaps.



There is some air leakage around where the oil monitor enters and exists the wall in the basement. Adding silicon or a small amount of spray foam will help seal this leak.



I was not able to access this area but the closet across from the furnace room seemed to have air leakage when I was performing the blower door test. I think the air leak is coming from the vent on the wall that has a pink piece of rigid board insulation stuffed in it. 



</airSeal>
                <ceilings>The insufficient amount of insualtion in the ceiling is causing for lots of heat loss in the winter. Adding at least R-10 or R-20 blown in cellulose would help keep the heat in the home better.  Spray Foaming around the exterior of the attic could help with tthermal bridging (where the wall and ceiling meet) as well.</ceilings>
                <windows>The wooden framed windows on the right and left sides of the house in the basement are outdated and could be upgraded to Energy Star windows rated for zone 3. The window in the basement bedroom on the back that is partially smashed is leaky around the edges and the broken glass is reducing the insualtion value of the window as well. I would make this window a priority to replace over the other two. Though they should still be replaced eventually.</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>The furnace is about 25 years old now and is due for an upgrade. There are a couple options here:



Electric Furnace:



You can use existing ductwork. Will not have to worry about back drafting as no exhaust is needed for this and any chimney can be blocked off. Electricity is 100% efficient. 



Condensing Oil Furnace; 



You can use existing ductwork. You will  not need a chimney as this unit uses PVC pipe as an exhasut becuase the temperature of the exhaust is so low. The combustion air will be sealed off from the interior of the home and can avoid back drafting. 



Make sure that this furnace has a high quality heat exchanger and high static burner to aviod any soot build up in the exhaust and to avoid corrosion of the heat exchanger.



These Units are 90-95% efficient.



Condensing Propane Furnace:



You can use existing ductwork. You will  not need a chimney as this unit uses PVC pipe as an exhasut becuase the temperature of the exhaust is so low. The combustion air will be sealed off from the interior of the home and can avoid back drafting. 



You don't have to worry about corrosion or soot build up like you would with a condensing oil furnace (though this should not happen) as propane is easier to extract heat from than oil.



These units are 95% efficient.



Ways to Upgrade existing Furnace:



Have a brushless DC Motor Installed. This can reduce the average furnace electrical consumption by 70% or more.

Seal all joints in ductwork with a water based duct mastic (sealant) or a high temperature tape. The tape tends to degrade and permit air leakage over time compared to the sealant.



</HeatingSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
